  "lean": {
    "statement": "∀ {E : Type u_1}\n  [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] [inst_1 : InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [inst_2 : FiniteDimensional ℝ E]\n  [inst_3 : MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E] (x y : E) (τ : ℝ),\n  0 < τ →\n    have G := fun a => MeasureTheory.Measure.map (fun z => a + √τ • z) (ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E);\n    have L := fun z => Real.exp (Inner.inner ℝ (x - y) (z - y) / τ - ‖x - y‖ ^ 2 / (2 * τ));\n    Measurable L ∧\n      (G x = (G y).withDensity fun z => ENNReal.ofReal (L z)) ∧\n        (G x).AbsolutelyContinuous (G y) ∧ (G x).rnDeriv (G y) =ᵐ[G y] fun z => ENNReal.ofReal (L z)",
    "statement_sha256": "6b5054a2f8fbe4dadaeff23c9c1439bd39f3f13dd8bad3d065314ca3c727a332",
    "compiled": true,
    "approved_definition_context": [
      "NormedAddCommGroup and InnerProductSpace over real scalars with FiniteDimensional form a finite-dimensional real inner-product space; BorelSpace gives its Borel measurable structure. Dimension zero is allowed.",
      "stdGaussian E is the canonical centered standard Gaussian probability law on E. Measure.map is pushforward, so map(z to a+sqrt(tau) smul z) is an actual random-variable law.",
      "Measure.withDensity f multiplies the base measure by the extended nonnegative measurable density f. AbsolutelyContinuous mu nu means mu is absolutely continuous with respect to nu, in that direction.",
      "Measure.rnDeriv mu nu is the extended nonnegative Radon-Nikodym derivative dmu/dnu. Equality with the explicit density is asserted almost everywhere under the measure displayed.",
      "ENNReal.ofReal embeds the nonnegative part of a real into extended nonnegative reals. ENNReal.toReal is the real conversion, totalized at infinity; use only the actual displayed hypotheses and conclusions when interpreting it.",
      "Integrable means almost-everywhere strong measurability and finite integral of norm. The displayed integral is the real Bochner integral. Real.log is the real logarithm, and powers with real q are real powers.",
      "The inner product and norm are actual inner-product-space structures, and smul is real scalar multiplication. Lets define actual laws and likelihoods; all quantifiers and parameter restrictions are as printed."
    ]
  },
